1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,800
Section 3

2
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:02,329
Tutor:

3
00:00:02,329 --> 00:00:02,431
Ah ...

4
00:00:02,431 --> 00:00:04,100
come in, Russ.

5
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:04,427
Russ:

6
00:00:04,427 --> 00:00:05,900
Thank you.

7
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:06,443
Tutor:

8
00:00:06,443 --> 00:00:10,743
Now you wanted to consult me about your class presentation on nanotechnology - you're

9
00:00:10,743 --> 00:00:14,300
due to give it in next week, aren't you?

10
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:14,561
Russ:

11
00:00:14,561 --> 00:00:15,342
That's right.

12
00:00:15,342 --> 00:00:16,669
And I'm really struggling.

13
00:00:16,669 --> 00:00:20,392
I chose the topic because I didn't know much about it

14
00:00:20,392 --> 00:00:22,085
and wanted to learn more,

15
00:00:22,085 --> 00:00:26,823
but now I've read so much about it, in a way there's too much

16
00:00:26,823 --> 00:00:31,223
to say - I could talk for much longer than the twenty minutes I've

17
00:00:31,223 --> 00:00:31,900
been allocated.

18
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:35,355
Should I assume the other students don't know much,

19
00:00:35,355 --> 00:00:38,426
and give them a kind of general introduction,

20
00:00:38,426 --> 00:00:43,800
or should I try and make them share my fascination with a particular aspect?

21
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:44,119
Tutor:

22
00:00:44,119 --> 00:00:45,394
You could do either,

23
00:00:45,394 --> 00:00:48,900
but you'll need to have it clear in your own mind.

24
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:49,362
Russ:

25
00:00:49,362 --> 00:00:52,600
Then I think I'll give an overview.

26
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:53,044
Tutor:

27
00:00:53,044 --> 00:00:53,488
OK.

28
00:00:53,488 --> 00:00:59,700
Now, one way of approaching this is to work through developments in chronological order.

29
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:00,400
Russ:

30
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:01,100
Uh-huh.

31
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:01,459
Tutor:

32
00:01:01,459 --> 00:01:06,482
On the other hand, you could talk about the numerous ways that nanotechnology is

33
00:01:06,482 --> 00:01:07,200
being applied.

34
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:07,648
Russ:

35
00:01:07,648 --> 00:01:13,470
You mean things like thin films on camera displays to make them water-repellent,

36
00:01:13,470 --> 00:01:17,500
and additives to make motorcycle helmets stronger and lighter.

37
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:18,005
Tutor:

38
00:01:18,005 --> 00:01:18,510
Exactly.

39
00:01:18,510 --> 00:01:25,576
Or another way would be to focus on its impact in one particular area,

40
00:01:25,576 --> 00:01:26,586
say medicine,

41
00:01:26,586 --> 00:01:28,100
or space exploration.

42
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:28,485
Russ:

43
00:01:28,485 --> 00:01:31,177
That would make it easier to focus.

44
00:01:31,177 --> 00:01:33,100
Perhaps I should do that.

45
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:33,422
Tutor:

46
00:01:33,422 --> 00:01:36,000
I think that would be a good idea.

47
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:36,508
Russ:

48
00:01:36,508 --> 00:01:37,017
Right.

49
00:01:37,017 --> 00:01:42,100
How important is it to include slides in the presentation?

50
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:42,474
Tutor:

51
00:01:42,474 --> 00:01:44,717
They aren't essential, by any means.

52
00:01:44,717 --> 00:01:49,952
And there's a danger of tailoring what you say to fit whatever slides you

53
00:01:49,952 --> 00:01:50,700
can find.

54
00:01:50,700 --> 00:01:55,600
While it can be good to include slides, you could end up spending too

55
00:01:55,600 --> 00:01:57,350
long looking for suitable ones.

56
00:01:57,350 --> 00:02:00,500
You might find it better to leave them out.

57
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:00,902
Russ:

58
00:02:00,902 --> 00:02:01,707
I see.

59
00:02:01,707 --> 00:02:05,730
Another thing I was wondering about was how to start.

60
00:02:05,730 --> 00:02:10,959
I know presentations often begin with 'First I'm going to talk about this,

61
00:02:10,959 --> 00:02:13,373
and then I'll talk about that',

62
00:02:13,373 --> 00:02:18,200
but I thought about asking the audience what they know about nanotechnology.

63
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:18,505
Tutor:

64
00:02:18,505 --> 00:02:22,777
That would be fine if you had an hour or two for the presentation,

65
00:02:22,777 --> 00:02:27,049
but you might find that you can't do anything with the answers you get,

66
00:02:27,049 --> 00:02:30,100
and it simply eats into the short time that's available.

67
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:30,524
Russ:

68
00:02:30,524 --> 00:02:36,453
So, maybe I should mention a particular way that nanotechnology is used, to focus

69
00:02:36,453 --> 00:02:37,300
people's attention.

70
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:37,875
Tutor:

71
00:02:37,875 --> 00:02:39,600
That sounds sensible.

72
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:39,958
Russ:

73
00:02:39,958 --> 00:02:42,821
What do you think I should do next?

74
00:02:42,821 --> 00:02:46,400
I really have to plan the presentation today and tomorrow.

75
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:46,784
Tutor:

76
00:02:46,784 --> 00:02:52,161
Well, initially I think you should ignore all the notes you've made, take a

77
00:02:52,161 --> 00:02:53,698
small piece of paper,

78
00:02:53,698 --> 00:02:58,307
and write a single short sentence that ties together the whole presentation:

79
00:02:58,307 --> 00:03:03,300
it can be something as simple as 'Nanotechnology is already improving our lives'.

80
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:06,061
Then start planning the content around that.

81
00:03:06,061 --> 00:03:10,400
You can always modify that sentence later, if you need to.

82
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:11,200
Russ:

83
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:12,000
OK.

84
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:12,427
Tutor:

85
00:03:12,427 --> 00:03:16,269
OK, now let's think about actually giving the presentation.

86
00:03:16,269 --> 00:03:22,246
You've only given one before, if I remember correctly, about an experiment you'd been

87
00:03:22,246 --> 00:03:23,100
involved in.

88
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:23,629
Russ:

89
00:03:23,629 --> 00:03:24,686
That's right.

90
00:03:24,686 --> 00:03:26,800
It was pretty rubbish!

91
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:27,163
Tutor:

92
00:03:27,163 --> 00:03:31,158
Let's say it was better in some respects than in others.

93
00:03:31,158 --> 00:03:36,242
With regard to the structure, I felt that you ended rather abruptly, without rounding

94
00:03:36,242 --> 00:03:36,968
it off.

95
00:03:36,968 --> 00:03:40,600
Be careful not to do that in next week's presentation.

96
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:41,450
Russ:

97
00:03:41,450 --> 00:03:42,300
OK.

98
00:03:42,300 --> 00:03:45,947
Tutor:And you made very little eye contact with the audience,

99
00:03:45,947 --> 00:03:49,926
because you were looking down at your notes most of the time.

100
00:03:49,926 --> 00:03:54,568
You need to be looking at the audience and only occasionally glancing at your

101
00:03:54,568 --> 00:03:54,900
notes.

102
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:55,600
Russ:

103
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:56,300
Mmm.

104
00:03:56,300 --> 00:03:56,591
Tutor:

105
00:03:56,591 --> 00:03:58,631
Your body language was a little odd.

106
00:03:58,631 --> 00:04:02,420
Every time you showed a slide, you turned your back on the audience

107
00:04:02,420 --> 00:04:06,500
so you could look at it - you should have been looking at your laptop.

108
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:08,367
And you kept scratching your head,

109
00:04:08,367 --> 00:04:12,722
so I found myself wondering when you were next going to do that, instead

110
00:04:12,722 --> 00:04:14,900
of listening to what you were saying!

111
00:04:14,900 --> 00:04:15,255
Russ:

112
00:04:15,255 --> 00:04:15,965
Oh dear.

113
00:04:15,965 --> 00:04:18,448
What did you think of the language?

114
00:04:18,448 --> 00:04:21,997
I knew that not everyone was familiar with the subject,

115
00:04:21,997 --> 00:04:25,900
so I tried to make it as simple as I could.

116
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:26,275
Tutor:

117
00:04:26,275 --> 00:04:26,650
Yes,

118
00:04:26,650 --> 00:04:27,775
that came across.

119
00:04:27,775 --> 00:04:31,900
You used a few words that are specific to the field,

120
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:34,525
but you always explained what they meant,

121
00:04:34,525 --> 00:04:37,900
so the audience wouldn't have had any difficulty understanding.

122
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:38,600
Russ:

123
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:39,300
Uh-huh.

124
00:04:39,300 --> 00:04:39,634
Tutor:

125
00:04:39,634 --> 00:04:43,311
I must say the handouts you prepared were well thought out.

126
00:04:43,311 --> 00:04:45,986
They were a good summary of your presentation,

127
00:04:45,986 --> 00:04:49,329
which people would be able to refer to later on.

128
00:04:49,329 --> 00:04:51,000
So well done on that.

129
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:51,567
Russ:

130
00:04:51,567 --> 00:04:52,700
Thank you.

131
00:04:52,700 --> 00:04:53,064
Tutor:

132
00:04:53,064 --> 00:04:56,700
Well, I hope that helps you with next week's presentation.

133
00:04:56,700 --> 00:04:57,143
Russ:

134
00:04:57,143 --> 00:04:58,471
Yes, it will.

135
00:04:58,471 --> 00:04:59,800
Thanks a lot.

136
00:04:59,800 --> 00:05:00,220
Tutor:

137
00:05:00,220 --> 00:05:04,000
I'll look forward to seeing a big improvement, then.
